# Line-ending policy.
#
# The repository stores every text file with LF in the object database, and the
# working tree is LF too - that is what rustfmt, cargo and the CI runners write,
# and it is the only convention that keeps the checkout byte-identical to the
# index on every platform. Without this file the checkout follows the host's
# core.autocrlf, so a Windows clone silently gets CRLF working-tree files while
# `git diff` still reports them as unmodified; the first tool that rewrites one
# of those files (rustfmt, an editor) then flips it to LF and the file shows up
# as fully modified. Pinning `eol=lf` here removes that mismatch.
#
# `text=auto` makes Git normalise line endings on the way in, so a commit made
# from a CRLF working tree still stores LF. `eol=lf` then forces the checkout to
# LF as well.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Windows-only scripts. Command Prompt and PowerShell tolerate LF in most cases,
# but `.bat` parsing has known traps around LF-only line endings and `gradlew.bat`
# is already stored as CRLF in the index, so keep these as CRLF explicitly.
*.bat text eol=crlf
*.cmd text eol=crlf
*.ps1 text eol=crlf

# Not text: never touched by end-of-line conversion. Listed explicitly (rather
# than relying on auto-detection) so that a future binary format is not
# accidentally normalised.
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.jpeg binary
*.webp binary
*.ico binary
*.icns binary
*.gif binary
*.svg  text
*.jar binary
*.dll binary
*.exe binary
*.so binary
*.dylib binary
*.bin binary
